# Governance Metadata Conventions

> **FAIR:** [`fairness_metadata.mdc`](fairness_metadata.mdc) — F2-02 via `make validate-fair-metadata`; schema via `make validate-metadata-files-content`; metadata ↔ SQL via `make validate-lineage-consistency`; lake I1-01 after deploy.

## The Golden Rule
Every `.sql` file in `queries/{layer}/` **must** have a matching `.yml` in `metadata/{layer}/` with the same base name. CI fails without it.

## Common Required Fields (all layers)

```yaml
database_name: str          # e.g. "enrich_ebdb_proposal", "dw_rent", "metric_rent"
table_name: str             # table name without prefix
description: str            # min 10 characters
domain: str                 # must match valid domain (see below)
owner: str                  # email: user@quintoandar.com.br
columns:
  column_name:
    description: str        # min 10 characters
```

> PII classification is **not** part of metadata authoring yet — see
> [Personal Data Handling](#personal-data-handling) below.

**Valid domains:** must **full-match** one value below (same list as F2-01 / CI Yamale — canonical source: [`fairness_metadata.mdc`](fairness_metadata.mdc) and `METADATA_DOMAIN_CI_ALLOWLIST_PATTERN` in `fairness_assessment/constants.py`):

`Agents`, `Atlas DB`, `Broker XP`, `Conversational XP`, `Cross`, `Data Life Cycle`, `Data Ops & Governance`, `Data Platform`, `DS Pricing`, `Fintech`, `For Rent`, `For Sale`, `Growth`, `House and Listing`, `International`, `Journey Optimizer`, `MLOps`, `People`, `QCX`, `Rede`, `Support and Services`, `Tech Platform`

**Deprecated labels (do not use in new metadata):** `Governance` → `Data Ops & Governance`; `Platform` → `Data Platform` or `Tech Platform`; `Data Science` → `DS Pricing` or `MLOps`; `Support and Service` → `Support and Services`.

## Personal Data Handling

### PII classification in metadata — not available yet

Do **not** add a `personal_data_classification` key to columns — the metadata validator rejects it and CI/lint fails. Do **not** encode a column's PII tier in its `description` either. Document columns functionally, like any other column.

A formal PII-classification field in bietlejuice metadata is planned but not implemented yet.

### Personal Data Catalog (reference only)

These tiers come from the QuintoAndar Personal Data Catalog. They are **not** written into metadata YAML today — they inform which LGPD controls apply (below) and will become metadata fields in a future rollout.

- **`sensitive`** — special-category data under LGPD Art. 11: racial/ethnic origin, political opinion, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, union membership, health information, biometric data. Must never appear in metric/qube outputs or publicly-accessible schemas without documented LGPD approval.
- **`highly_personal`** — financially or legally sensitive data: bank statement, credit history, credit score, IRPF, INSS statement, criminal background, card numbers, photo of person with ID document. Prefer hashing or masking before promoting to enrich/dw.
- **`personal`** — standard personal data under LGPD Art. 5: name, CPF, date of birth, email, phone, address, RG, passport, geolocation, salary, device ID, IP, cookies, etc.

### LGPD controls — enforced today

These controls apply **independently of metadata classification**. Until `personal_data_classification` exists, determine sensitivity from:

1. **Documented domain exceptions** (e.g. fintech schemas in `sql_conventions.mdc` §13)
2. **Column semantics** in the SQL (CPF, health data, credit score, etc.)
3. **Governance review** when uncertain — do **not** invent metadata classification to satisfy a control

| Control | When it applies | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| No raw PII in enrich/DW | New tables; use `sk_person` / join `dim_person` | SQL (`sql_conventions.mdc` §13) |
| `table_privileges` | Table carries `sensitive` or `highly_personal` data | DAG declaration |
| `privacy.k_anonymity ≥ 5` | Metric/qube output derived from `sensitive` data | Qube/metric declaration |
| `has_right_to_be_forgotten` guard | Reverse DAG exporting contact PII | SQL (`sql_conventions.mdc` §13) |

### Examples

**Metadata** — describe PII columns functionally; no classification field or PII-tier text:

```yaml
columns:
  cpf:
    lineage:
      - ebdb_public.person.cpf
    description: "Brazilian individual taxpayer identification number (CPF)."
```

**Declaration** — `table_privileges` lives in the declaration, not in metadata:

```yaml
# fintech/cyber_*_declaration.yml
tables_customization:
  contracts:
    table_privileges:
      data-platform-engineers: ["SELECT"]
```

## Enrich & DW Columns — `lineage` required

`lineage` is required for every column whose value **derives from a source column**. It is **not** required for columns whose value is a hardcoded literal in the SQL (e.g. `'RENT' AS business_context`, `'FS_DILIGENCE' AS entity`, `'OWNER' AS persona`). Hardcoded values have no source column to trace.

```yaml
columns:
  id_contract:
    lineage:
      - datalake_ebdb_clean.contract.id    # format: database.table.column
    description: "Unique identifier for the contract."
  amount:
    lineage:
      - datalake_ebdb_clean.contract.rent_amount
    description: "Monthly rent amount in BRL."
    categories:                            # optional: for categorical columns
      ACTIVE: Currently active contract.
      CANCELED: Contract was canceled.
  business_context:
    # No lineage — hardcoded literal value ('RENT') in the SQL.
    description: "Business context. Always RENT for this entity."
```

## Metric Columns — `dimension` or `metric` block required
Each metric column must be **either** a dimension or a metric, never plain:

```yaml
# Dimension column (used to slice/group the metric)
week:
  lineage:
    - dw_public.dim_date.week_start
  description: "Week start date."
  dimension: true

# Metric column (the measurable value)
ongoing_contracts:
  description: "Count of unique contracts active during the week."
  metric:
    name: Ongoing Contracts Weekly
    description: "Count of unique contracts that were active each week."
    acronym: OC
    is_additive: false
    business_stage: Demand          # e.g. Supply, Demand, Transaction, Retention
    hierarchy: User analysis
    approved_by: owner@quintoandar.com.br
    link_to_metric: "https://quintoandar.sa.looker.com/looks/1234"  # optional
```

## Raw vs clean (Woodpecker / Yamale — not the same)

| | **raw** (`raw_schema.yml`) | **clean+** (`clean_schema.yml`, core, enrich, dw, metric) |
|--|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| Table `description` | Optional (Yamale) | Required, min 10 chars (Yamale). **FAIR F2-01:** non-empty; write substantive business context (entity/process, grain, main consumers) — table TDQ is advisory at PR time |
| `owner` | Optional | Optional in schema; required for FAIR F2-01 when file is in PR diff |
| `columns` | Optional | Required |
| Column `description` | Optional (Yamale) | Required, min 10 chars (Yamale). **FAIR F2-02 (blocking on clean+):** substantive — business meaning, units/format, derivation logic; not name echo, status-only labels, or platform filler |
| `lineage` | Not in schema | Required on enrich/dw for derived columns |
| `tags` on columns | Allowed (e.g. Confidential) | — |

**Description quality (all layers where you document):** Yamale enforces **presence and min length** on clean+; FAIR enforces **substance**. Table and column text should explain *what* the data represents in business terms, *how* it is produced or derived, and *who* uses it — aligned to [`fairness_metadata.mdc`](fairness_metadata.mdc) and [`fair_requirement_glossary.md`](../../docs/governance/fair_requirement_glossary.md) (F2-01 / F2-02). Min 10 characters alone is not enough for clean+ columns in PR CI.

Many **raw** tables are ingested via **Spark jobs** without `queries/raw/*.sql` — lineage-consistency does not apply there. Prefer rich documentation on **clean / enrich / dw**; touch **raw** only when that YAML is in the PR diff or CI failed on it. See `fair-metadata/reference/woodpecker_layer_gates.md` and `fairness_metadata.mdc`.

## Validation Commands
```bash
# Check that every SQL file has a matching metadata file
make validate-metadata-files-exist

# Check that metadata content is valid against schema
make validate-metadata-files-content

# Check SQL lineage consistency with metadata
make validate-lineage-consistency
```

## Common Mistakes
- `description` shorter than 10 characters → CI fails on **clean+**, not on raw (raw table description is optional in Yamale).
- Bulk-editing `metadata/raw/` with owner/domain/column text copied from clean when raw is not in the PR diff → unnecessary; Woodpecker would not require it.
- `owner` not matching email regex → CI fails.
- Missing `lineage` on an enrich/dw column that derives from a source → CI fails.
- Adding `lineage` to a hardcoded literal column (e.g. `'RENT' AS business_context`) → incorrect; remove it.
- Metric column without `dimension: true` or `metric:` block → CI fails.
- Metric SQL referencing raw/clean tables directly → lineage validation fails.
- Adding a `personal_data_classification` key to any column → **CI/lint fails**; the validator does not accept it yet. Remove it. (Don't move the classification into `description` either — PII classification isn't part of metadata authoring yet.)
- Storing raw PII (name, CPF, email, address, etc.) as a column in a new enrich/dw table instead of joining `dim_person` → governance review will block it.
- `sensitive` or `highly_personal` data in enrich/dw without `table_privileges` in the declaration → LGPD violation risk (infer tier from column semantics or domain rules — not from a metadata field).
- `sensitive` data exposed in a metric/qube output without `k_anonymity ≥ 5` → LGPD violation risk.
